Yes, the main experience is a chat interface, similar to ChatGPT or Claude. It differs in two ways. A graph panel shows what the agent retrieved, and there is a separate page for adding repositories. The user first connects a codebase, then asks questions about it in chat.

Your project doesn't get installed inside the other project. It runs as its own app, and the user points it at a codebase to ingest, as you'd add a project to a tool like SonarQube. There are a few ways to do that.

1. Git URL (best default)
The user pastes https://github.com/org/repo on the Repositories page, and the backend clones it and starts ingestion.

Public repos work with no setup.
For private repos, the user supplies a personal access token or deploy key, which you store as an environment variable or encrypted setting.

2. Local folder (best for developers trying it on their own code)
Because the backend runs inside Docker, it can't see the host's filesystem by default. The user mounts their project into the container, read-only:

yaml
# docker-compose.yml
backend:
  volumes:
    - ${CODEBASE_DIR:-./sample-repo}:/workspace/repo:ro

They set CODEBASE_DIR=/path/to/my/project in .env, and the UI then offers /workspace/repo as a source. The :ro flag means the tool can never modify their code.

3. Zip upload (optional)
The user drags a zip into the UI and the backend unpacks it into a temp folder. It's easy for non-technical users, but it's a convenience feature, so skip it in v1.

Typical flow

The user opens the Repositories page and clicks "Add repository".
They pick Git URL or local folder.
POST /repos starts a background ingestion job: Tree-sitter parses the code, Neo4j gets the graph, and Qdrant gets the embeddings.
A progress bar shows the status (parsing, building graph, embedding).
When it reads "Ready", the user switches to the chat and asks questions.

Keeping the index fresh

v1: a manual "Re-index" button. Hash each file so only changed files are reprocessed (ING-7 in the PRD).
Later: a webhook from GitHub, a post-commit git hook, or a small CLI (explorer sync .) that CI can call after each merge. A GitHub App that comments the impact analysis on pull requests would make a strong future feature.

The tool should only read and parse the code, never run it. Say so in your README, since people will be wary of pointing a tool at their private code.

For the first version I'd support Git URL and local mount, since together they cover most users. I can write the ingestion endpoint and the docker-compose.yml for that setup if you'd like.